  {
    "page": 2,
    "bbox": [
      72.0999984741211,
      73.40000915527344,
      279.3839416503906,
      172.6000213623047
    ],
    "text": "This is an example of columns. With \ncolumns, the page is split into two or \nmore horizontal sections. Unlike \ntables, in which you usually read \nacross a row and then down to the \nnext, in columns, you read down a \ncolumn and then across to the next."
  },
  {
    "page": 2,
    "bbox": [
      324.1000061035156,
      79.40000915527344,
      536.7741088867188,
      178.6000213623047
    ],
    "text": "When columns are not created \ncorrectly, screen readers may run \nlines together, reading the first line of \nthe first column, then the first line of \nthe second column, then the second \nline of the first column, and so on. \nObviously, that is not accessible."
  }
